## 🔍 CODE REVIEW CHECKLIST

### Before Submitting Code:
- [ ] Code follows project style guide
- [ ] All TypeScript errors resolved
- [ ] No console.log statements left
- [ ] Proper error handling added
- [ ] Complex logic has comments
- [ ] Accessibility labels added
- [ ] Performance considerations addressed
- [ ] No hardcoded values (use constants)
- [ ] Proper null/undefined checks
- [ ] Tests added for new functionality
- [ ] Variable/function names are descriptive
- [ ] No code duplication (DRY principle)

### Reviewing Others' Code:
1. **Be constructive** - Suggest improvements, don't just criticize
2. **Ask questions** - "Why did you choose this approach?"
3. **Praise good code** - Acknowledge well-written solutions
4. **Focus on important issues** - Don't nitpick style if tools handle it
5. **Test the code** - Actually run it if possible

## 📊 CODE METRICS

### Complexity Guidelines:
- **Function length**: Aim for < 50 lines, max 100 lines
- **File length**: Aim for < 300 lines, max 500 lines
- **Parameters**: Max 3-4 parameters (use object for more)
- **Nesting depth**: Max 3 levels deep
- **Cyclomatic complexity**: Aim for < 10

### Refactoring Indicators:
- Function is hard to name clearly
- Function has more than one responsibility
- Code needs extensive comments to understand
- Duplicated code in multiple places
- Changes in one place require changes elsewhere
- Tests are difficult to write

## 🎯 QUALITY METRICS

### Definition of Done:
A feature is "done" when:
- [ ] Functionality implemented and working
- [ ] TypeScript types properly defined
- [ ] Error handling implemented
- [ ] Loading states handled
- [ ] Accessibility implemented
- [ ] Performance optimized
- [ ] Code reviewed and approved
- [ ] Tests written and passing
- [ ] Documentation updated
- [ ] No linter errors
- [ ] Works on iOS and Android

---

**Remember**: Code is read far more often than it is written. Write for the next developer, who might be you in 6 months.
